{"record": "end", "run_id": "20261112T180805Z_dev-02_staged_abbf", "reason": "answered", "detail": "", "answer": {"answer": "No. The two analyses use different thresholds. The dSph limit is a one-sided 95% limit. It is the cross section where 2[ln L_max − ln L] = 2.71, i.e. a TS drop of 2.71. The AMS-02 antiproton limit is the upper end of the ΔQ ≤ 3.84 region, where Q = −2 ln L and ΔQ is measured relative to the minimum over non-negative rates. The paper says this differs from the usual one-sided 2.71 prescription. The profiled nuisance parameters also differ. The dSph analysis profiles over each dwarf's J-factor, using a Gaussian prior in log10 J with the measured uncertainty. The antiproton analysis profiles over the Galactic diffusive-halo half-height L. It uses a Gaussian penalty in log10 L, centred on 4.96 kpc with σ = 0.197, and profiles over 1–12 kpc.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.2"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.3"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "5"}]}, "turns": 5, "seconds": 12.705, "tool_calls": 5, "tool_result_chars": 39438, "input_tokens": 46637, "output_tokens": 875, "parser_versions": ["3"], "ended_at": "2026-11-12T18:08:18+00:00"}
